llvm-3.3 backport patch: the stack protector's analysis lays out the protected frame (PS4)

This patch CHANGES CODE GENERATION for every function that carries a stack
canary, and only for those: the layout of an unprotected frame stays index
order. The StackProtector pass becomes an analysis that classifies each
alloca it protects (a large array: an array, or a structure holding one, of
ssp-buffer-size bytes or more, or an alloca of that size or of a variable
size; a small array; an object whose address is taken under the strong
rules), and the prolog and epilog inserter and the local stack slot pass
take that classification instead of MachineFrameInfo's MayNeedSP flag,
which goes. In this patch only the large arrays move next to the guard (each
in frame-index order); llvm-3.3-ssplayout-02 adds the other two groups. It
ports three upstream commits by Josh Magee, all after the 3.3 branch:

  r191570  8ecfb52388e1  2013-09-27  "[stackprotector] Refactor the
           StackProtector pass from a single .cpp file into StackProtector.h
           and StackProtector.cpp."
  r193653  3f1c0e35e639  2013-10-29  "[stackprotector] Update the
           StackProtector pass to perform datalayout analysis."
  r197653  22b8ba2d6769  2013-12-19  "[stackprotector] Use analysis from the
           StackProtector pass for stack layout in PEI and LocalStackSlot
           passes."

Adapted to 3.3: the class in the new include/llvm/CodeGen/StackProtector.h
is 3.3's own (it keeps the TargetLoweringBase constructor, reads
SSPBufferSize from the TargetMachine options and builds the Triple where 3.3
did, and still inserts the guard as the 3.3 pass does; the upstream parents
had moved to a TargetMachine, r184349, to the stack-protector-buffer-size
attribute, r186217 and r186863, and to the intrinsic check of r188766, none
of which is ported). PEI::getAnalysisUsage is in ShrinkWrapping.cpp in 3.3
(upstream r193749 of 2013-10-31 moved it), so the requirement is added
there. The callers and passes in the targets this build leaves out (MBlaze,
Hexagon, NVPTX) are not touched. r199716 (the checked return of the buffer
size attribute) has nothing to apply to in 3.3. X86FastISel records the
guard's frame index as upstream does, which concerns -O0 only.
Moving the pass after addPreISel changes nothing on X86, which adds no
pre-ISel pass. The PS4 compiler is of this generation (D32's bracket: after
r196925 of 2013-12-10, before r206214 of 2014-04-14), and the base's frames
follow the rule (docs/DECISIONS.md D35).

